# The spellings this project's documentation uses, and why each one is here.
#
# docs-scan reads this file and nothing else does. An entry is one line holding
# a spelling that is refused, then `->`, then the spelling to write instead. The
# line immediately above an entry is its reason, and docs-scan refuses an entry
# that has none, so a word cannot join this list without somebody writing down
# what it is for.
#
# WHAT THE LIST IS FOR is that the words in the documentation stay the words in
# the product. An operator reading two documents that name one thing two ways
# has to work out whether they are the same thing, and that work falls on the
# reader who trusted the documents most. Where the product has an object with a
# name, the documentation calls it that name.
#
# THE LIST HOLDS THE VOCABULARY THE DOCUMENTATION ALREADY ESTABLISHES. docs-scan
# refuses an entry whose answer no tracked document uses, so a spelling cannot
# be required here before anything has been written in it. That fails closed in
# the other direction too: taking the last use of a word out of the
# documentation reddens the entry that answers with it, and the repair is to
# take the entry out in the same change rather than to leave a rule nobody
# writes to.
#
# WHERE A WORD IS SOMEBODY ELSE'S, put it in backticks or in an indented block.
# A check name, a field in another project's metadata and a package's own name
# are not this project's prose and are not judged, which is what already keeps
# `Analyze (csharp)` in docs/quality-parity.md readable: it is the name of a
# check on another repository and is quoted rather than spelled the way this
# tree would spell its own.
#
# ONE WORD THIS PROJECT SPELLS ITS OWN WAY IS NOT HERE, and the reason is worth
# reading before adding it. This tree writes `licence` in its own prose, and the
# file at the root, the name of the licence itself and the field in a package's
# metadata all spell it the other way. Refusing that spelling would refuse
# NOTICE.md, which quotes text this project did not write, and it would refuse
# the sentence in README.md naming the licence. A rule reading bytes cannot tell
# a proper name from prose, so the word stays out of the list and a reader is
# what holds it.
#
# A PLURAL IS ITS OWN ENTRY. Nothing here knows any English morphology, and a
# check guessing at it would refuse `buses` for `bus`. Where both the singular
# and the plural are plausible, both are written down.

# The object is named in docs/model.md as the organisation, so the other
# spelling is that object's own name written a second way.
organization -> organisation

# The plural of the entry above, which docs/model.md also uses.
organizations -> organisations

# The spelling SECURITY.md and the change log record use for what a thing does
# under a condition, which is the word a threat model turns on.
behavior -> behaviour

# The plural of the entry above, which docs/decisions/0005-money-and-time.md
# uses where it says that two ways of valuing a historical pipeline are both
# legitimate.
behaviors -> behaviours

# docs/quality-parity.md calls the tool #110 asks for a second static analyser
# with a different lens, and the documentation arriving with it will name it.
analyzer -> analyser

# What docs/model.md does to a name before it decides whether two records are
# the same person, and the spelling the identity rule is written in.
normalized -> normalised

# The spelling docs/decisions/0008-workflow.md and docs/required-checks.md use
# for a thing being known for what it is.
recognized -> recognised

# The change log is two words in every document that names it and in the record
# that decides it, and it is a thing in this product rather than a file at the
# root of a repository, which is what the other spelling usually means.
changelog -> change log

# The client is named in docs/decisions/0009-web-client.md as the web client,
# two words, and the milestone that builds it uses the same two.
webclient -> web client

# One word, which is what docs/model.md uses where it makes an address part of
# the identity rule.
e-mail -> email

# A change lands as a pull request and the documentation says so in full. The
# abbreviation reaches somebody who has not met it yet, which is exactly the
# reader an operator-facing document is written for.
PR -> pull request
